Phase 10 proof run — 2026-09-18, AFTER the production deploy.
================================================================================

WHAT THIS IS, AND WHAT IT IS NOT

  The owner applied 20261215000000_phase10_cancellation_vocabulary and
  20261215000100_phase10_obligation_unique to production at 02:36 UTC on 2026-09-18
  WITHOUT a preflight, because there was no docs/transaction-flow/phase-10-proof/ on
  the branch to run one from. Phase 1, phases 3-9 and migration-110 all ship a proof
  package. Phase 10 did not. The STOP 2 report described the sequence — "preflight.sql
  in full before, prisma migrate status to show what will apply, and both verification
  halves after" — and the files behind that sentence were never written.

  THIS LOG THEREFORE RECORDS AN EXERCISE, NOT A DEPLOY. Nothing here ran against
  production. Everything below ran against a throwaway loopback PostgreSQL 16.13
  cluster at 127.0.0.1:5432, database `txflowpre`, built for this purpose.

  DEGRADED, and stated rather than skipped: the server available here is PostgreSQL
  16.13, not CI's 17.6 (no Docker daemon in this environment). CI's `migrations` job
  remains the authority for chain replay, and it passed on this branch.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
BUILDING THE PRE-DEPLOY STATE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  The two Phase 10 migration directories were moved aside, `prisma migrate deploy`
  was run against an empty `txflowpre`, and they were moved back. `git status` on
  frontend/prisma/migrations was clean afterwards — the directories are byte-identical
  to HEAD and nothing in the chain was edited.

  THE RESULT MATCHES THE OWNER'S PRODUCTION CENSUS, taken immediately before the run:

    ledger                      119 distinct applied      (production: 121 rows /
                                                           119 distinct / 2 rolled
                                                           back / 0 stuck — the two
                                                           extra ROWS are retry
                                                           history from an earlier
                                                           phase, which is why every
                                                           assertion counts finished,
                                                           non-rolled-back rows)
    AuctionInvitationStatus     10 labels: QUEUED, SENT, DELIVERED, OPENED, BOUNCED,
                                DECLINED, RESPONDED, OFFER_SUBMITTED, EXPIRED,
                                REPLACED                  — no CANCELLED
    PickupStatus                10 labels: NOT_SCHEDULED, PROPOSED, DEALER_COUNTERED,
                                SCHEDULED, CHECKED_IN, COMPLETED, RESCHEDULED,
                                EXCEPTION, NO_SHOW, RELEASED
                                                          — no CANCELLED
    post_completion_obligations post_completion_obligations_pkey,
                                post_completion_obligations_deal_id_idx
                                                          — the partial unique absent

  AND ONE READING THE OWNER ASKED TO BE RECORDED:

    AuctionStatus               PENDING, ACTIVE, CLOSED, EXPIRED, CANCELLED, REOPENED

    REOPENED IS A REAL LIVE LABEL. The first independent review of the decline-route
    change caught `closeAuction` guarding on a hand-written [PENDING, ACTIVE] that
    omitted it; this is that catch confirmed against a database built from the
    committed chain, and the owner confirmed the same six labels in production. The
    review was right against the schema, not only against the code.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FIRST RUN — preflight.sql against the PRE-deploy state
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  psql -X -P pager=off -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 --single-transaction \
       -c "SET TRANSACTION READ ONLY" -f preflight.sql

  ALL 20 ROWS CHECKED. No BLOCK. The chain assertion read

    chain is at the verified pre-state (119 distinct applied names)
      | 119 distinct / 119 rows / 0 rolled back | CHECKED

  which is production's pre-deploy distinct-applied count exactly. Had this file
  existed at 02:36, it would have passed cleanly and the run would have proceeded —
  that is the point of writing it against the pre-state rather than the state
  production is in now.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SECOND RUN — verify.sql against the PRE-deploy state (the NEGATIVE direction)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  A verify file that cannot fail is the defect phase-8's shipped with: every row it
  printed was true and it would have reported a clean deploy with half the migrations
  missing. So verify.sql was run BEFORE the migrations, where every object it asserts
  is absent.

  10 of 19 assertions reported MISSING, and they are exactly the created objects:

    MISSING   AuctionInvitationStatus carries CANCELLED
    MISSING   PickupStatus carries CANCELLED
    MISSING   index post_completion_obligations_open_deal_type_key
    MISSING   ...is VALID and UNIQUE
    MISSING   ...predicate is the partial carve-out §13-D60 ruled
    MISSING   ...is keyed on (deal_id, type)
    MISSING   ledger 20261215000000_phase10_cancellation_vocabulary
    MISSING   ledger 20261215000100_phase10_obligation_unique
    MISSING   ledger has exactly one applied row per phase 10 migration
    MISSING   chain is at the phase 10 post-state (121 distinct applied names)

  PRESENT    the three pre-existing columns, the pre-existing deal_id index, the
             three contrast labels (EXPIRED / NOT_SCHEDULED / RESCHEDULED), the
             duplicate check, and "no stuck migrations"

  Every assertion that SHOULD fail failed, and every assertion about something that
  pre-dates the phase held. The file discriminates.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THIRD RUN — the duplicate, and the migration failing on it for real
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Phase 9's equivalent assertion shipped reading "0 of 0" — it passed while checking
  nothing, which is §8.1h's class arriving inside the file written to catch it. So
  this one was exercised against real rows.

  Seeded on the loopback cluster (users -> buyers -> vehicle_requests ->
  vehicle_request_offers -> deals -> obligations; `deals` carries a CHECK that
  requires an offer lineage, which is why the fixture goes through a vehicle-request
  offer):

    o_p10proof_1  d_p10proof  TEMP_TAG  PENDING
    o_p10proof_2  d_p10proof  TEMP_TAG  PENDING     <- two OPEN rows, one (deal, type)

  preflight.sql then reported:

    duplicate OPEN (deal_id, type) pairs (what CREATE UNIQUE INDEX fails on)
      | 1 | BLOCK
    post_completion_obligations rows | 2 total, 2 open | CHECKED

  AND THE MIGRATION GENUINELY FAILED ON IT, which is what makes the assertion worth
  having rather than a hypothesis:

    Applying migration `20261215000000_phase10_cancellation_vocabulary`
    Applying migration `20261215000100_phase10_obligation_unique`
    Error: P3018 ... Database error code: 23505
    ERROR: could not create unique index
           "post_completion_obligations_open_deal_type_key"
    DETAIL: Key (deal_id, type)=(d_p10proof, TEMP_TAG) is duplicated.

  Note what that left behind: the FIRST migration applied and the second did not, so
  the chain was genuinely stuck mid-phase. Running preflight.sql against that state
  produced SIX distinct BLOCK conditions, every one of them a real fault rather than a
  contrived one:

    BLOCK   AuctionInvitationStatus does not already carry CANCELLED
              | ALREADY PRESENT — added out of band?
    BLOCK   PickupStatus does not already carry CANCELLED
              | ALREADY PRESENT — added out of band?
    BLOCK   duplicate OPEN (deal_id, type) pairs | 1
    BLOCK   ledger: 20261215000000_... not yet applied | ALREADY APPLIED
    BLOCK   stuck migrations | 20261215000100_phase10_obligation_unique
    BLOCK   chain is at the verified pre-state (119 distinct applied names)
              | 121 distinct / 121 rows / 0 rolled back

  NOT EXERCISED, and named rather than implied: the four assertions that fire when an
  object is ABSENT — the two enum TYPES, the obligations table, its three columns, and
  the RESOLVED label on PostCompletionObligationStatus — were not exercised, because
  removing them means dismantling a schema the rest of the chain depends on. They are
  structural existence checks of the same shape as the ones that DID fire, and they
  are the four this log cannot claim to have watched fail.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FOURTH RUN — recovery, and verify.sql against the POST-deploy state
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Recovery followed the path an operator would take:

    UPDATE post_completion_obligations SET status='RESOLVED' WHERE id='o_p10proof_2';
    prisma migrate resolve --rolled-back 20261215000100_phase10_obligation_unique
    prisma migrate deploy                     -> All migrations have been successfully
                                                 applied.

  ALL 19 ASSERTIONS PRESENT. No MISSING.

  AND THE LEDGER SHAPE IS PRODUCTION'S. The rolled-back marker from the recovery left
  the loopback at

    122 rows / 121 distinct / 1 rolled back

  against production's

    123 rows / 121 distinct / 2 rolled back

  — different row counts, same distinct-applied count, and BOTH read PRESENT. That is
  the "retry history is not a fault" rule doing its job: the ledger assertions count
  finished, non-rolled-back rows, so a rolled-back row sitting beside a success does
  not read as a missing migration. The exercise produced that shape by accident and it
  is the most useful thing in this log.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FIFTH RUN — the index's SEMANTICS, not just its existence
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  §13-D60 ruled a PARTIAL unique "so a RESOLVED obligation does not bar a legitimately
  recurring one". verify.sql asserts the predicate is present; that is not the same as
  asserting it behaves. Three inserts against the post-state:

    (a) a SECOND open obligation on the same (deal, type)
          -> REFUSED
             ERROR: duplicate key value violates unique constraint
                    "post_completion_obligations_open_deal_type_key"
             DETAIL: Key (deal_id, type)=(d_p10proof, TEMP_TAG) already exists.

    (b) resolve the live one, then insert a recurring obligation on the SAME pair
          -> ALLOWED (INSERT 0 1)

    (c) a SECOND RESOLVED row on the same (deal, type)
          -> ALLOWED (INSERT 0 1)

  Idempotency is enforced where the ruling said, and the carve-out is real: a full
  unique would have refused (b) and (c) and made a second temp tag on one deal
  impossible for ever.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THIS PACKAGE DOES NOT CLAIM
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  - It did not gate the production run. Nothing here did. The production apply at
    02:36 UTC was verified by the owner independently, and those readings are what
    verify.sql asserts.
  - It ran on PostgreSQL 16.13, not production's server version.
  - The four absence assertions named under THIRD RUN were never watched to fail.
  - `rollback.sql` in each migration directory is a DOCUMENTED REVERSE, not a tested
    one: the enum half cannot be reversed at all (PostgreSQL cannot drop an enum
    label) and says so in the file rather than pretending otherwise.
